Module 01 — Introduction to Kali Linux & Linux Fundamentals

Lab Objective

Set up a pre-built Kali Linux virtual machine and become familiar with the Kali Linux environment, terminal, shell, privileges, and fundamental Linux file-system operations.

Environment

Kali Linux VM
VirtualBox
Linux Shell / Terminal

🔹 1. Deploying the Kali Linux VM

I deployed the pre-built Kali Linux virtual machine and launched it in VirtualBox.

The initial setup involved:

Importing the pre-built VM
Starting the Kali Linux environment
Familiarizing myself with the desktop and terminal
Understanding how the VM interacts with the host machine

🔹 2. Exploring Root Privileges

I explored the concept of root privileges in Linux and how they differ from those of a normal user.

This helped me understand why certain administrative operations require elevated privileges and why root access should be handled carefully.

🔹 3. Getting Familiar with the Shell

I practiced working directly from the Kali Linux terminal and became familiar with basic shell interaction.

I also practiced useful keyboard shortcuts to improve terminal navigation and command-line efficiency.

🔹 4. Basic Linux Commands

I practiced fundamental commands for navigating and interacting with the Linux system.

Some of the areas covered included:

pwd
ls
cd

These helped me understand my current location, view directory contents, and navigate through the filesystem.

🔹 5. Command Documentation

I learned how to find information about Linux commands instead of relying on memorization.

For example:

man <command>

This introduced me to Linux manual pages and the importance of being able to independently research command options and usage.

🔹 6. Creating & Navigating Directories

I practiced creating directories and moving between them using commands such as:

mkdir
cd

This helped me become more comfortable navigating the Linux filesystem from the command line.

🔹 7. Redirecting Command Output

I practiced redirecting command output into files instead of displaying it directly in the terminal.

I also learned the difference between overwriting and appending output.

command > file.txt
command >> file.txt

> redirects output and overwrites the file, while >> appends the output to the existing file.

🔹 8. File & Directory Management

I practiced managing files and directories from the terminal, including:

Creating files/directories
Deleting files/directories
Moving files
Moving directories

Commands covered included:

rm
rmdir
mv
 What I Took Away

This lab gave me my first hands-on experience working with Kali Linux from the command line. Rather than just learning Linux commands theoretically, I practiced using the shell to navigate the filesystem, manage files, redirect output, and work with different privilege levels.

These fundamentals will form the basis for the more security-focused labs that follow.